## Summary
The article discusses differences in the timeframes for evaluating the performance of high-salary CEOs across countries. In Korea, performance is assessed over a 3-year period, whereas in the US and UK, it is considered over 5 to 10 years.

## Key Points
- Korea evaluates high-salary CEO performance over a 3-year period.
- The US and UK use a 5-10 year period for CEO performance evaluation.
- There is international variation in CEO assessment durations, which may reflect different corporate governance practices.
- Longer evaluation periods in some countries could support long-term strategic planning.
- This discrepancy might influence compensation structures and executive accountability.


